114427	114453	114462	114463	the baronet	13871	" Now , my dear fellow , be a good boy , and learn your lesson well . You will soon meet with your reward . "
114454	114460	114482	114482	Chichester	527	" I am all attention , "
114465	114480	114482	114482	Chichester	527	" In the first place I shall show you how to _ secure _ , "
114484	114566	114576	114577	the baronet	13871	" and as you know the game of _ Hazard _ well enough , I need say but little more on that head . There are two ways of _ securing _ . The first is to hold one of the dice between the fore and middle fingers , or the middle and third fingers , against the side of the box , so that one finger must cover the top of the die -- in this way , you see . "
114570	114574	114625	114625	Chichester	527	" I understand , "
114605	114623	114625	114625	Chichester	527	" This system is not so easy as the second , which I shall presently show you , "
114627	114721	114733	114734	the baronet	13871	" because the die must be kept cleverly inside the box , so as not to be seen . The second way of _ securing _ is by taking hold of one of the dice by the little finger , and keeping it firm against the palm of the hand while you shake the box , so as to be able to drop it skilfully upon the table at the proper moment , when it will seem as if it came from the box along with the other . This is the way . "
114725	114731	114733	114734	the baronet	13871	" I shall soon understand , "
114736	114760	114772	114772	Chichester	527	" Of course by being able to _ secure _ one die , you may make it turn up any number you choose . "
114761	114770	114772	114772	Chichester	527	" When you mean to practise this dodge , "
114774	114880	114905	114906	the baronet	13871	" call five for _ a main _ ; because you can _ secure _ the four , and there is only the six on the loose die that can come up against you . If you have a good stake to get , _ secure _ a five every time ; because when the _ main _ is six to five , or seven to five , or eight to five , or nine to five , or ten to five , you _ must _ win every time , because you ca n't possibly throw out while the five is _ secured _ . "
114881	114903	114916	114916	Chichester	527	" But will not the ear tell the _ pigeon _ that there is only one die rattling in the box ? "
114908	114914	114916	114916	Chichester	527	" Look at this box , "
114918	114943	114956	114957	Sir Rupert	528	" It has two rims cut inside , near the bottom : the one die shaking against them produces the sound of two dice . "
114944	114954	114916	114916	Chichester	527	" Are there not some peculiarities about these dice ? "
114972	115063	115074	115075	the baronet	13871	" Yes -- those are _ unequal dice _ , and are so well made that no one , except a regular sharper , could detect them . They are bigger at one end than the other , and the sixes are placed on the smaller squares , because you must play with these dice to win upon high numbers , which are on those smaller squares . The dice will in nine cases out of ten fall upon the larger squares , and thus show the high numbers uppermost . "
115067	115072	115088	115088	Chichester	527	" And these dice ? "
115082	115086	115088	115088	Chichester	527	" Loaded ones , "
115090	115113	115125	115126	Sir Rupert	528	" These are to throw low ; and so the two sides which have got four and five on them are loaded . "
115117	115123	114916	114916	Chichester	527	" How are they loaded ? "
115128	115246	115252	115253	the baronet	13871	" The corner pip of the four side , next to the five side , is bored very neatly to a certain depth : the same is done to the corner pip of the five side , adjoining the four side . Thus the two holes , so bored , meet each other at right angles . One of the holes is covered over with some strong cement : quicksilver is then poured in ; and the other hole is covered over with the cement . The spots are blackened ; and your dice are ready for use . These being intended to throw low , you must call a main , and take the odds accordingly . "
115247	115250	115252	115253	the baronet	13871	" Well , "
115255	115292	115303	115303	Chichester	527	" I think I can now safely say that I know enough of the elements of your grammar to enable me to practise myself . Let us devote half an hour to the working of cards . "
